Figure 14.1: Structure of a VAE: the encoder outputs the mean and standard deviation of a normal distribution, which is sampled to obtain the input of the decoder. Source: https://towardsdatascience.com/understanding-variational-autoencoders-vaes-f70510919f73







if the pixels are normalized between 0 and 1 and considered as Bernoulli variables, as in the original paper, the decoder has a logistic output layer and the reconstruction loss is the binary cross-entropy (Cross-entropy);
if the decoder outputs the mean of a normal distribution with a fixed variance, the negative log-likelihood is, up to constants, the mean square error between the input and its reconstruction (Mixture density networks for continuous distributions).

in variational autoencoders, the reconstruction loss is an expectation over the encoder q_\phi(z | x) (Variational autoencoders);
in variational inference in general, when the ELBO of an arbitrary model has no closed form and must be estimated with samples of the approximate posterior;
when the latent variables are discrete, for example categories or tokens, which cannot be reparameterized as a differentiable function of a noise;
in reinforcement learning, where an agent controls the distribution of its own experiences through its behavior, and wants to change this behavior to obtain better outcomes.
